      "rev_n_2_e50e06_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_e50e06_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_e50e06",
        "thought_text": "The error in the previous reasoning was that the function was returning `right` instead of `left` at the end. This caused the function to return an incorrect maximum height for some inputs. The correct approach is to return `left` because it represents the largest value that satisfies the condition of having fewer or exactly the required number of cubes.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_e50e06_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_e6039fba22",
        "created_at": 1794530505.3661907
      },
      "rev_n_3_533c16_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_533c16_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_533c16",
        "thought_text": "The error in the previous reasoning was that the loop stops one element early, resulting in an incorrect height. To fix this, we need to adjust the condition in the while loop to ensure it continues until we can't add the next level. This can be done by changing the condition to `cubes_used + (height + 1) * (height + 2) // 2 <= n`, which correctly calculates the total cubes needed for the next level.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_533c16_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_ae876489d6",
        "created_at": 1794530522.7679367
